Over all measurable classifiers, the infimum of the classification errors is defined as the Bayes error and is denoted by R^*. One can construct the unique measurable classifier that attains the Bayes error. It is called the Bayes classifier and is denoted by g^*. One can show that the Bayes classifier g^* is defined as follow:g^*(x) = {\left\lbrace \begin{array}{ll}
1 &\quad \textnormal { if } \eta (x)\ge \frac{1}{2}\\
-1 &\quad \textnormal { otherwise }
\end{array}\right.}where \eta (x) is the conditional probability that the label y is equal to 1, given an observation x.Since a given model \mathcal {G} is by construction a subset of all measurable functions, the excess error of a classifier g \in \mathcal {G} is defined as the discrepancy between the misclassification error of g \in \mathcal {G} and the Bayes error. Thus, the problem of classification defined in section is equivalent to the minimization of the excess error over all g \in \mathcal {G}. For g \in \mathcal {G}, the difference R(g) - R^* can be decomposed as follow:R(g) - R^* = R(g) - \inf _{g \in \mathcal {G}} R(g) \, + \, \inf _{g \in \mathcal {G}} R(g) - R^*.The first difference on the right hand side of the equal sign is referred to as the estimation error while the second difference is known as the approximation error. The estimation error measures the quality of the classifier g with respect to the optimal misclassification error of \mathcal {G} while the approximation error measures how well the optimal classification error of \mathcal {G} can approximate the Bayes error. The decomposition of the excess error in terms of the estimation and approximation errors shows that the choice of the model \mathcal {G} is subject to a trade-off: a rich model is more likely to have a small approximation error but at the price of a larger estimation error, and vice-versa. Note that since both the joint probability distribution P(x,y) and the conditional distribution P(\, . \, | \, x) are unknown, both g^* and R(g) are unknown. Even with various noise assumptions, estimating the approximation error is difficult . On the other hand, the estimation error can be bounded.

